PccPerPixelGridPlacement: keep the per-face depth it measured

WHY (Jahshaka lane R5-ROOM, 2026-09-15; owner+lead joint rule "no room in any
definition", PHOTON_SPEC §13). The renderer must decide, per probe, whether that
probe saw anything worth photographing -- a probe whose six faces see nothing but
sky reproduces, badly and with a grid seam, what the sky cubemap already holds
perfectly, and costs a cube capture, its share of the probe array and the
per-pixel probe loop to do it.

`PccPerPixelGridPlacement::buildEnd` ALREADY takes that measurement: one averaged
1x1 depth value per cube face (`0.5 * fDist / fApproxDist`, saturated at 1, where
fApproxDist is the distance from the probe's camera to the full region's own
face). It uses the six values to fit the probe's shape and then throws them away.

The fitted shape cannot be read backwards into them. It is padded by 1 %, snapped
to the full region (`snapToFullRegion`), snapped to the grid's outer sides
(`snapToSides` -- a tolerance relative to the WHOLE region span, which on the
outer faces of a coarse grid is the dominant term), and it saturates at twice the
region distance for anything the probe could not see. A host reading the shape
therefore cannot tell "this probe sees a wall a little inside the region" from
"this probe sees nothing at all and was snapped back to the region" -- which is
exactly the distinction it has to make.

So the measurement is KEPT instead of discarded: six floats per probe, in
CubemapSide order, each the averaged distance that face saw as a multiple of the
distance to the region's own face (1 = on the region's face, 2 = nothing within
twice it). Nothing existing changes: the array is filled where the values are
already in a local, and the fit is byte-for-byte the code it was.

Upstream-worthy as it stands (an accessor for a measurement the class already
takes), and offered as such.

SOURCE patch: every tree must re-run build-ogre.sh.

